of varying lengths.\ndf['log_loc'] = np.log(df['loc'] + 1)", "# design_vs_code_complexity\n# Design complexity as a fraction of overall cyclomatic complexity.\n# Usefulness: Highlights how much of a module’s complexity derives from design decisions rather than its logic, aiding maintainability analysis.\ndf['design_vs_code_complexity'] = df['iv(g)'] / (df['v(g)'] + 1e-5)", "# normalized_operands\n# Density of total operands relative to unique operands.\n# Usefulness: Measures operand reuse patterns, where higher values may indicate redundancy and potential for inefficiencies in code.\ndf['normalized_operands'] = df['total_Opnd'] / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1e-5)", "# intelligence_ratio\n# Intelligence per Halstead effort.\n# Usefulness: Indicates how efficiently effort is translated into readable and maintainable code, which correlates with defect rates.\ndf['intelligence_ratio'] = df['i'] / (df['e'] + 1e-5)", "# rolling_mean_v\n# Rolling mean of Halstead volume (window=3).\n# Usefulness: Captures local trends in code volume, highlighting areas with increasing or decreasing complexity.\ndf['rolling_mean_v'] = df['v'].rolling(window=3, min_periods=1).mean()", "# rolling_mean_e\n# Rolling mean of Halstead effort (window=3).\n# Usefulness: Identifies local effort spikes, providing insight into periods of inconsistent effort that might lead to defects.\ndf['rolling_mean_e'] = df['e'].rolling(window=3, min_periods=1).mean()", "# loc_to_code_comment_ratio\n# Ratio of lines of code (loc) to lines of code and comment (locCodeAndComment).\n# Usefulness: Highlights the proportion of uncommented code, which could signal maintainability and defect risks.\ndf['loc_to_code_comment_ratio'] = df['loc'] / (df['locCodeAndComment'] + 1e-5)", "# comment_blank_ratio\n# Ratio of lines of comments to blank lines.\n# Usefulness: Indicates balance between documentation and formatting practices, identifying areas where comments might be insufficient.\ndf['comment_blank_ratio'] = df['lOComment'] / (df['lOBlank'] + 1e-5)", "# v(g)_to_branch_ratio\n# Ratio of cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) to branch count.\n# Usefulness: Captures decision-making density, highlighting overly complex branching structures that may lead to defects.\ndf['v(g)_to_branch_ratio'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['branchCount'] + 1e-5)", "# effort_intelligence_ratio\n# Ratio of Halstead effort to intelligence.\n# Usefulness: Quantifies inefficiencies in effort relative to intelligent code design, potentially signaling areas prone to defects.\ndf['effort_intelligence_ratio'] = df['e'] / (df['i'] + 1e-5)", "# difficulty_per_line\n# Halstead difficulty normalized by lines of code (loc).\n# Usefulness: Provides a measure of average difficulty per line, helping detect modules with disproportionate complexities.\ndf['difficulty_per_line'] = df['d'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)", "# opcode_reuse_ratio\n# Ratio of total operators to unique operators.\n# Usefulness: High values indicate repetitive operator usage, which could imply inefficiencies or redundant patterns in code.\ndf['opcode_reuse_ratio'] = df['total_Op'] / (df['uniq_Op'] + 1e-5)", "# operand_reuse_ratio\n# Ratio of total operands to unique operands.\n# Usefulness: Highlights reuse patterns in operand usage, where high values may suggest inefficiencies or low-quality code.\ndf['operand_reuse_ratio'] = df['total_Opnd'] / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1e-5)", "# rolling_std_v\n# Rolling standard deviation of Halstead volume over a window of 3.\n# Usefulness: Detects variations in code size over short sequences, revealing potential inconsistencies in size and complexity.\ndf['rolling_std_v'] = df['v'].rolling(window=3, min_periods=1).std()", "# rolling_std_e\n# Rolling standard deviation of Halstead effort over a window of 3.\n# Usefulness: Identifies fluctuations in effort requirements, showing deficiencies in the coherence of the development process.\ndf['rolling_std_e'] = df['e'].rolling(window=3, min_periods=1).std()", "# volume_effort_ratio\n# Ratio of Halstead volume to effort.\n# Usefulness: Validates the alignment between code size and its required effort; large deviations might signal inefficiency or defects.\ndf['volume_effort_ratio'] = df['v'] / (df['e'] + 1e-5)", "# loc_effort_ratio\n# Effort required per line of code.\n# Usefulness: Measures how evenly distributed the effort is across lines of code, revealing potential hotspots.\ndf['loc_effort_ratio'] = df['e'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)", "# loc_volume_ratio\n# Halstead volume per line of code.\n# Usefulness: Highlights the intensity of computation within a single line, aiding in identifying complexity hotspots.\ndf['loc_volume_ratio'] = df['v'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-5)", "# cumsum_effort\n# Cumulative sum of Halstead effort across the dataset.\n# Usefulness: Tracks total effort accumulated across modules, highlighting those contributing disproportionately to overall effort.\ndf['cumsum_effort'] = df['e'].cumsum()", "# cumsum_volume\n# Cumulative sum of Halstead volume across the dataset.\n# Usefulness: Indicates total accumulated code complexity, identifying areas with disproportionate contributions to overall complexity.\ndf['cumsum_volume'] = df['v'].cumsum()", "df.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)"]
